{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answers\":[{\"question_id\":\"Q017_prp8_evolutionary_mosaic\",\"answer\":\"In SCOPe 2.08, PDB 3jb9 chain A is decomposed into five Prp8/Spp42 domains ordered along the sequence: d3jb9a5, A:47-697, is the Prp8 N-terminal alpha+beta domain and defines the SCOPe fold d.394; d3jb9a4, A:698-822, is placed in the bromodomain family a.29.2.1; d3jb9a3, A:823-1604, is the Prp8 reverse-transcriptase-like domain in the DNA/RNA polymerases superfamily e.8.1; d3jb9a2, A:1605-1780, is a Prp8 endonuclease-like domain in the restriction-endonuclease-like superfamily c.52.1; and d3jb9a1, A:1784-2030, is a Prp8 beta-finger domain-like member of the RNase H-like superfamily c.55.3. The decomposition therefore treats Prp8 as an evolutionary mosaic: four pieces fall in previously characterized bromodomain, polymerase, restriction endonuclease-like, and RNase H-like structural/evolutionary neighborhoods, whereas the large N-terminal domain was not assigned to an older fold and instead starts the Prp8/Spp42 N-terminal fold d.394. These conclusions are per-domain: the fold and superfamily assignments do not make the five domains mutually homologous, do not transfer intact polymerase, nuclease, RNase H, or bromodomain reader activity to Prp8 without active-site and biochemical evidence, and do not justify using one of the five domains as a template for the whole protein.\",\"evidence_used\":[\"mcp__scope.get_scop_domains\",\"/api/v1/releases/current\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a1\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a1/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a2\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a2/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a3\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a3/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a4\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a4/parents\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a5\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3jb9a5/parents\"]}]}"}}
